DOMAIN: The Theses, Jetztzeit, and Messianic Time
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-06-10
PAPERS_FOUND: 17 total (High: 8, Medium: 6, Low: 2, INCOMPLETE: 5)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CrossRef
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
Benjamin's "On the Concept of History" (1940, also known as the "Theses
on the Philosophy of History") is the textual core of his philosophy of
history. Written in the final months of his life under the shadow of
fascism and the Molotov-Ribbentrop Pact, its eighteen theses (plus
appendices) constitute a compressed, fragmentary assault on historicism,
social-democratic progressivism, and the concept of homogeneous, empty
time. The central temporal concepts—Jetztzeit (now-time), the dialectical
standstill (Stillstand), the tiger's leap (Tigersprung), the constellation
(Konstellation), and the "weak messianic power" (schwache messianische
Kraft)—have generated an enormous secondary literature that divides roughly
into three camps: (1) theological or messianic readings that foreground
Jewish mysticism and the Kabbalah (Scholem, Wohlfarth, Moses); (2)
materialist-Marxist readings that emphasize the historical materialist
framework and the critique of progress (Tiedemann, Lowy, Tomba); and (3)
hybrid or third-position readings that insist on the constitutive tension
between theology and materialism as the productive core of Benjamin's
thought (Fenves, Khatib, Deuber-Mankowsky, Desideri).

Jetztzeit designates the eruption of a qualitatively charged "now" into
the flow of chronological time—a moment in which past and present collide
in a "dialectical image," making the past legible and politically actionable.
This is opposed to the "homogeneous, empty time" of historicism, in which
events are strung together like beads on a rosary, subject to progressive
accumulation. The "weak messianic power" (Thesis II) names the claim that
each generation inherits a small, non-triumphalist capacity to redeem
suffering generations of the past—not through eschatological fulfillment
but through the revolution of recognition. The figure of the "tiger's leap
into the past" (Thesis XIV) articulates revolutionary action as a citing of
past struggles in a new context.

Recent scholarship has expanded the domain in several directions: comparative
readings of Benjamin alongside Paul (Agamben), alongside Derrida (Levine,
Sahraoui), and alongside Cohen's neo-Kantian messianism (Desideri,
Deuber-Mankowsky). There has also been renewed attention to the monadological
structure of Jetztzeit—the idea that each historical moment contains within
it the compressed totality of history—and to the spatial implications of
Benjamin's temporal concepts (Desideri 2016).

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain is the textual foundation for the entire literature review:
all major debates in Benjamin scholarship (the Arcades Project, the angel
of history, dialectical images, redemption and revolution) ultimately refer
back to the compressed arguments of the Theses. Covering this domain
thoroughly provides the conceptual vocabulary on which synthesis writers
will depend for all other domains.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Tiedemann's original German-language interpretation remains among the most
precise philological readings but is less accessible than the English
secondary literature; the Lowy book remains the most sustained commentary
but lacks abstract verification. Recent work on Benjamin's influence on
decolonial theory (Ross 2024, Vaki 2024) is included in other domains.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Organize around the core opposition (homogeneous time vs. Jetztzeit), then
treat the messianic complex (weak power, tradition of the oppressed, tiger's
leap), then address the materialist/theological debate, and finally note
recent comparative expansions.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Theological/messianic readings: 4 papers — foreground Kabbalah, Jewish mysticism, eschatology
- Materialist/Marxist readings: 3 papers — emphasize critique of progress, historical materialism
- Hybrid/tension readings: 5 papers — treat theology-materialism tension as constitutive
- Comparative readings (Paul, Derrida, Cohen): 4 papers — expand messianic concept beyond Benjamin
====================================================================
}

@book{lowy2005firealarm,
  author = {Löwy, Michael},
  title = {Fire Alarm: Reading Walter Benjamin's ``On the Concept of History''},
  year = {2005},
  publisher = {Verso},
  address = {London and New York},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Löwy offers a thesis-by-thesis commentary on ``On the Concept of History,'' arguing that Benjamin's text articulates a revolutionary-romantic critique of the ideology of progress, combining Jewish messianism and libertarian Marxism into a philosophy of the ``emergency brake'' of revolution. The book insists that Benjamin's text is politically urgent rather than merely academically interesting, reading each thesis as a position in contemporary political debates.

  RELEVANCE: This is the most sustained English-language commentary on the Theses and is indispensable for any treatment of Jetztzeit, the weak messianic power, and the tiger's leap. Löwy's reading foregrounds the anti-progressivist politics of the text and the figure of redemption-as-revolutionary-rupture, providing a benchmark against which other interpretations define themselves.

  POSITION: Materialist-romantic reading that resists both purely theological and purely Marxist reductions.
  },
  keywords = {Benjamin, Theses-on-History, Jetztzeit, messianic-time, historical-materialism, critique-of-progress, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{khatib2013messianic,
  author = {Khatib, Sami},
  title = {The Messianic Without Messianism: Walter Benjamin's Materialist Theology},
  journal = {Anthropology \& Materialism},
  year = {2013},
  volume = {1},
  doi = {10.4000/am.159},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Khatib argues that Benjamin's messianism should not be read as theological content but as a structural feature of his materialist philosophy of history: the messianic names an irreducible non-identity within historical time, a negativity that cannot be appropriated by either secular or religious tradition. The ``messianic without messianism'' is a form of immanent critique that keeps open the possibility of rupture without positing any transcendent guarantor.

  RELEVANCE: This article provides the most precise conceptual analysis of what ``weak messianic power'' means for the overall architecture of Benjamin's philosophy of history, distinguishing it from both Agambenian appropriation and Derrida's messianicity. It directly addresses the question of how the messianic functions within the Theses as a structural rather than theological category.

  POSITION: Hybrid reading that insists on the constitutive indistinction of materialism and theology in Benjamin.
  },
  keywords = {Benjamin, messianic, materialism, theology, Jetztzeit, Theses-on-History, High},
  abstract = {This article discusses Walter Benjamin’s messianic thought. Far from being a theological concept proper or a secularized motif of Judeo-Christian religion, the messianic is a complex figure of thought addressing a dimension of profane life that is neither culture nor nature but a ‘weak power’ within history allowing for a messianic standstill of the self-totalizing and self-eternalizing progress of capitalist ‘real-history’. If the messianic is not about religion or political theology but concerns the profane order of the profane, how are we to conceive of it in a non-reductionist way? The underlying question of this article hence is: if the messianic is the theological name for something within the realm of the profane that is neither addressed by political philosophy nor life and social sciences, what is its relevance for a materialist understanding of history, historical time, and revolutionary politics?},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  sep_context = {SEP (Walter Benjamin article, section 5 "Politics"): "Against the receptions of Benjamin's politics outlined above, the messianic would rather appear to point to the 'irreducible theological remainder' upon which Benjamin's concept of redemption rests, one that Khatib (2013) argues should not be reduced to that of Judaeo-Christian theology."},
}

@book{fenves2010messianic,
  author = {Fenves, Peter},
  title = {The Messianic Reduction: Walter Benjamin and the Shape of Time},
  year = {2010},
  publisher = {Stanford University Press},
  address = {Stanford},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Fenves traces Benjamin's concept of time from its origins in phenomenology and Kantian critique, arguing that the notion of ``now-time'' (Jetztzeit) emerges from a sustained engagement with Husserlian time-consciousness and Kant's theory of practical reason. The ``messianic reduction'' names a procedure analogous to phenomenological reduction: it strips away the conventional temporal horizon to reveal the structure of the now as such.

  RELEVANCE: This book provides the most philosophically rigorous account of how Jetztzeit is generated within Benjamin's theoretical trajectory and why it cannot simply be identified with kairos or Pauline time-of-the-now. It is essential for understanding the distinctively philosophical (not only political or theological) stakes of Benjamin's temporal theory.

  POSITION: Philosophical-phenomenological reading that traces Benjamin's debt to Husserl and Kant.
  },
  keywords = {Benjamin, Jetztzeit, messianic-reduction, phenomenology, Kant, shape-of-time, High},
  abstract = {Asymptotics is the study of what happens in some mathematical domain, e.g. quantum theory, as one of its parameters tends to zero, e.g. as Planck’s constant h → 0, yielding Newtonian mechanics. Sometimes (mostly not) there are phenomena unique to the asymptotic domain near zero, e.g. so-called quantum chaos, and sometimes (mostly not) the behavior in the limit at zero is suddenly different, e.g. at h = 0 quantum theory becomes Newtonian - these are the interesting, and important, cases.

Batterman reviews asymptotics and discusses leading cases in detail, deriving many interesting mathematical and scientific consequences but also arguing important philosophical lessons concerning explanation, reduction and emergence. It is not hard to see a connection: reduction is about inter-theoretic relations and where these are controversial they are characterized by asymptotic relations, as in the quantum-Newtonian example above, while Batterman argues that the unique phenomena of the asymptotic domain present a new and important—perhaps the only real—kind of emergence. And since reduction is traditionally construed as a form of explanation, asymptotic explanation must be distinctive, and so it is argues Batterman, since it is concerned, not with detailed micro causal explanation but with what emerges when information is systematically excluded (via the terms involving the vanishing parameter).

The philosophical interest runs throughoutThe Devil in the Detailsas its chapter headings (following Ch.1 Introduction) indicate:

This is followed by a Conclusion (Ch.9), select bibliography and adequate index.

An asymptote in mathematics is a limiting value of a function, which is approached indefinitely closely but never quite reached, except by carrying some approximation process literally to infinity. Consider, for instance, the function y =1/x, x a real number; 1/x is always finite for x > 0, its limit as x becomes indefinitely large (x → ∞, ‘infinity’) is zero, Limx → ∞(1/x) = 0, and Limx → 0(1/x) = ∞. So there are two asymptotes for the hyperbolic curve y = 1/x: y = 0 (the x axis) and x = 0 (the y axis). An asymptotic domain refers to the region ‘near’ an asymptote and asymptotic theory is the (mathematical) theory of what happens in the asymptotic domain and ‘in the limit’ at the asymptote. In the case of 1/x, e.g., the asymptotic domains are the domains of very small and very large numbers and asymptotic theory would be the theory of the curve properties for these numbers. For 1/x, no new arithmetic phenomena emerge within the asymptotic domains, but there is a discontinuity at each of its asymptotes since there the laws of arithmetic break down.

In physics, on the other hand, we find that the most famous theory pairs are all asymptotically related - e.g. (and roughly) Lim1/c → 0(special relativity) → Newtonian mechanics, Limλ → 0(wave optics) → ray optics, Limh → 0(quantum mechanics) → Newtonian mechanics, etc. In the last two cases the asymptotic domains are filled with interesting phenomena, like rainbows in wave optics. (However, as Batterman notes at p.78, exact retrieval of a predecessor theory in the asymptotic limit rarely happens, there are typically formulae on both sides that don’t ‘match up’ asymptotically; one of the best fits is the Special Relativity-Newtonian mechanics pair, one of the most uneven fits is the statistical mechanics-thermodynamics pair, cf. Hooker 1981, Part I.) More abstractly, consider a “more fundamental theory” Tfand its coarser predecessor Tc, with Limp →0(Tf) = Tc, for appropriate p (1/c, λ, h, eTc.). There are three kinds of properties and relations, found in three separate domains, connected with this situation. In progression as p → 0, these are: the properties and relations of Tfin the non-asymptotic domain (p not small), those of the Tfasymptotic domain (p small), and those of Tc, the limit domain (p zero).
  abstract_source = {ndpr},

For the wave/ray optics example, the non-asymptotic domain is characterized by wave phenomena like diffraction and interference through superposition, the limit domain is characterized solely by linear rays, and the asymptotic domain by tiny wave patterns concentrated along curves constructed as the tangents to rays, called caustics. Here is the elegant Berry, a mathematical doyen of asymptotics, as quoted by Batterman (p.116):

This passage gives the sense of the caustic structure slowly becoming discernable, slowly emerging, from the interference patterns among waves as the wavelength decreases, until finally there is nothing left but the rays and their caustics.},
}

@article{deuber2008image,
  author = {Deuber-Mankowsky, Astrid},
  title = {The Image of Happiness We Harbor: The Messianic Power of Weakness in Cohen, Benjamin, and Paul},
  journal = {New German Critique},
  year = {2008},
  volume = {35},
  number = {3},
  pages = {57--69},
  doi = {10.1215/0094033X-2008-013},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Deuber-Mankowsky reads Benjamin's weak messianic power in parallel with Hermann Cohen's neo-Kantian messianism and Paul's theology of weakness, arguing that the ``image of happiness'' in Thesis II is not a theological residue but a constitutive philosophical concept: it designates the structural claim of past suffering on present action, articulated through the logic of citation and the constellation.

  RELEVANCE: This article illuminates the philosophical ancestry of Benjamin's concept of the weak messianic power and its connection to the concept of happiness (Glück), which is central to Theses I and II. It helps the reviewer understand what kind of ``power'' is at stake—one that does not operate through force but through the obligation of remembrance.

  POSITION: Hybrid reading that traces the neo-Kantian and Pauline inheritance of Benjamin's messianism.
  },
  keywords = {Benjamin, weak-messianic-power, Cohen, Paul, happiness, Theses-on-History, High},
  abstract = {In his book &lt;unl&gt;The Time That Remains: A Commentary on the Letter to the Romans&lt;/unl&gt;, Giorgio Agamben states that the famous hunchbacked dwarf from Walter Benjamin's first thesis on the philosophy of history was none other than Paul. Unlike Agamben, Benjamin was a reader of the Jewish philosopher Hermann Cohen, whose interpretation of human weakness represents not only an alternative but in many respects a counterposition to Paul. In my article I present Cohen's messianism and his generation of the concept of the individual out of the recognition of human weakness. I return then to the question of whether Benjamin in fact refers to the second letter to the Corinthians or whether there are not good reasons to assume that Benjamin is closer to Cohen's thought of the messianic power of weakness than to Paul's heroic suffering.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{desideri2016intermittency,
  author = {Desideri, Fabrizio},
  title = {Intermittency: The Differential of Time and the Integral of Space. The Intensive Spatiality of the Monad, the Apokatastasis and the Messianic World in Benjamin's Latest Thinking},
  journal = {Aisthesis. Pratiche, linguaggi e saperi dell'estetico},
  year = {2016},
  volume = {9},
  pages = {177--187},
  doi = {10.13128/AISTHESIS-18241},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Desideri argues that Benjamin's late conception of messianic time implies a monadological conception of space: the moment of now-time is not merely a point on a temporal line but a monad that ``contains the compressed history of all humanity'' (Thesis XVII). The figure of intermittence—the differential of time and the integral of space—captures the dialectical intertwining of continuity and discontinuity that structures Benjamin's conception of historical time. The real foil for Benjamin's late philosophy is not Heidegger but Hegel.

  RELEVANCE: This article addresses a relatively underexplored dimension of Jetztzeit—its spatial logic and its connection to the monad as a philosophical form—which is directly relevant to the question of how the tiger's leap ``into the past'' and the dialectical standstill work as spatiotemporal figures. It also provides important context for the Thesis XVII claim about now-time containing all of history.

  POSITION: Hybrid philosophical reading emphasizing the monadological and Origenian dimensions of Benjamin's messianism.
  },
  keywords = {Benjamin, Jetztzeit, monad, apokatastasis, messianic-world, intermittency, Medium},
  abstract = {The main topic of my paper concerns the theological-philosophical nexus between the intensive and qualitative spatiality of the Monad and the Origenian idea of Apokatastasis as a nexus that can clarify Benjamin's latest idea of the Messianic World. The first step will be, therefore, to explain (1) Benjamin's use of the Origenian notion of Apokatastasis in his Essay on Leskov and in the Passagenwerk. Secondly, I will discuss (2) how and to what extent such use is relevant for Benjamin's idea of Messianism. Thirdly, I will propose (3) the thesis that, for Benjamin, a messianic idea of time implies a monadological idea of space. In this regard, the relationship between continuity and discontinuity, which is crucial to understand the late Benjamin's idea of history, must be understood precisely as a dialectical relationship that involves not only time but also space. (4 ) The figure of intermittence – as we shall see – expresses in the most suitable way the intertwining of space and time as dialectically and  messianicaly oriented. This sequence of logical steps and critical considerations will shed light on the true nature of Benjamin's idea of dialectics and on its relationship to the idea of the totality. The real term of confrontation of Benjamin's late philosophy (from the Passagenwerk to the Theses on the concept of history) does not concern, therefore, Heidegger, but Hegel. In the light of this confrontation, Heidegger's Being and Time is already in pieces. It is as it were – we can say – behind Benjamin's movement of thought.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{desideri2015messianica,
  author = {Desideri, Fabrizio},
  title = {Messianica Ratio: Affinities and Differences in Cohen's and Benjamin's Messianic Rationalism},
  journal = {Aisthesis. Pratiche, linguaggi e saperi dell'estetico},
  year = {2015},
  volume = {8},
  pages = {133--145},
  doi = {10.13128/AISTHESIS-17570},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Desideri challenges Taubes's reading of Benjamin's theology as Gnosticism, arguing instead that Benjamin's messianism is a form of rational hope continuous with Cohen's neo-Kantian synthesis of Maimonides and Kant. Both share the reference to the a priori of justice and to Kantian rationality as the ground of hope; the difference lies in Cohen's ethical interpretation of the infinite task versus Benjamin's monadological-ontological reading.

  RELEVANCE: By situating Benjamin's messianic rationalism within the Cohen-Maimonides-Kant tradition, this article provides important background for understanding why the weak messianic power is weak in a philosophically specific sense—not eschatological force but rational obligation—and how Benjamin's conception differs from apocalyptic messianism.

  POSITION: Philosophical reading connecting Benjamin to neo-Kantian rationalism and Jewish philosophy.
  },
  keywords = {Benjamin, Cohen, messianic-rationalism, neo-Kantianism, Taubes, Jewish-philosophy, Medium},
  abstract = {In my paper, I intend firmly to criticize Taubes' interpretation of Benjamin's Theology as a modern form of Gnosticism (Benjamin as a modern Marcionit). In a positive way, I sustain rather the thesis that Benjamin's Messianism is in close connection with his conception of reason (“the sharpened axe of reason”) and, in particularly, with the paradoxical unity of Mysticism and Enlightenment, which, according to the famous definition of Adorno, distinguishes his thought. As a radically anti-magical and anti-mythical conception of the historical time, Benjamin's Messianism has to be considered as an original synthesis between motifs of the mystical tradition of the Jewish Kabbalah and motifs belonging to the rationalist tradition of the Jewish philosophy. Moving from Cohen's standpoint of a continuity between Maimonides and Kant, I consider therefore the affinity between his messianic conception of history and that of Benjamin. Both, Benjamin and Cohen, share, together with the reference to the a priori of the idea of justice, the reference to the Kantian connection between rationality and hope. Hence originates the non-eschatological Messianism of both. Motives of difference between Cohen and Benjamin’s messianic idea are to be found, conversely, in their different way to consider the idea of "the infinite task" and of its infinite fulfillment in the context of the historical time. Unlike the fundamentally ethical interpretation that Cohen gives of this relationship, Benjamin understands it ontologically in a monadological sense. This explains the constitutive relationship that exists, in Benjamin's philosophy, between Origin, Fragment and Revelation. In the light of this connection, Benjamin's messianic understanding of the historical time exceeds the Scholemian alternative between a restorative and a utopian conception of Messianism. Consequently, the Krausian motto “Ursprung ist das Ziel” (“The Origin is the Goal”) displays its truth in the idea of the messianic fragment or spark.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@book{moses2009angel,
  author = {Mosès, Stéphane},
  title = {The Angel of History: Rosenzweig, Benjamin, Scholem},
  year = {2009},
  publisher = {Stanford University Press},
  address = {Stanford},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Mosès reads Benjamin's philosophy of history alongside Rosenzweig and Scholem as three versions of a specifically Jewish modernity's response to the crisis of Enlightenment historicism. The Angel of History chapter on Benjamin analyses the figure of the angel in Thesis IX as a counter-model to historicist teleology: the angel sees only catastrophe where progress is proclaimed, and Benjamin's theory of now-time emerges as an attempt to arrest this catastrophe through the redemptive citation of the past.

  RELEVANCE: This book provides essential context for the theological dimension of Benjamin's messianism by situating it within the broader field of German-Jewish thought and the post-Enlightenment debate over messianic redemption. It is particularly important for understanding how Scholem's influence shapes (and was resisted by) Benjamin's conception of historical time.

  POSITION: Theological-messianic reading that foregrounds the Jewish philosophical context.
  },
  keywords = {Benjamin, Rosenzweig, Scholem, angel-of-history, Jewish-modernity, messianic-time, High},
  abstract = {Stéphane Mosès’sThe Angel of Historyis a classic in modern Jewish philosophy and a fine choice for the Stanford seriesCultural Memory in the Presentedited by Mieke Bal and Hent de Vries.The new translation by Barbara Harshav has made a meaningful contribution to this chain of tradition, carefully rendering complicated phrases from French that once served as interpretations in thought and deed from German. Since the first publication of this pioneering study in 1992, it is surprising to note how much has changed in the scholarship on Franz Rosenzweig, Walter Benjamin and Gershom Scholem. For one, it is no longer common to place Benjamin under the lens of Marxism. Equally, Rosenzweig is more commonly viewed in the light of Levinas, Expressionism and Heidegger today than in the shadow of Martin Buber. But perhaps even more, our picture of Scholem has considerably changed with the ongoing scholarship of the Kabbalah. We have generally come to see all three figures in their intellectual context more deeply rooted in modern German history and our understanding of the tensions evident in their work — between thought and action, rupture and causality, and indeed a Jew in a Christian world — has expanded considerably since the first publication of this book. The first English translation cannot help but raise questions regarding the study of modern German-Jewish thought two decades on.
  abstract_source = {ndpr},

The book is divided into three equal parts with each figure receiving three chapters, yet Rosenzweig emerges as the dominant figure. This is perhaps inevitable from a scholar whose earlier work entitledSystem and Revelationis a close reading of Rosenzweig’s magnum opus,The Star of Redemption. Still there are formal grounds for Rosenzweig’s preeminence. An exchange of letters from 1921 establishes the influence ofThe Star of Redemptionon Benjamin and Scholem. There is evidence to suggest that Benjamin shapes his early Messianism in relation toThe Star. Scholem’s debt to Rosenzweig is evident in many places, not least in a 1930 lecture delivered in Rosenzweig’s memory. With Rosenzweig as the benchmark, Mosès seeks to uncover the affinities between the three figures in the notions of history, time and language. He is the first to recognize the conceptual mutuality of the three authors and the need to understand them as part of a common tradition. However, in emphasizing their commonality, thematic structures meant to suggest fraternity occasionally ring hollow and the contrasts insufficient. For example, the association of the three with the categories of “religion”, “revolution” and “Zionism”, as Mosès suggests in the opening chapter, yields very little ground. Whereas the religion as leitmotif for Rosenzweig is self-evident, there is a very narrow road to revolution in Benjamin and little practical Zionism in Scholem. Much stronger is the notion of a common approach to history, which Mosès understands as a revolt against the idea of progress, a history leading to greater forms of reason that finds an epiphany in Hegel. As he remarks: “Past suffering is not abolished even by a triumphant future, which claims to give them meaning, and more than thwarted hopes are refuted by the failures that seem to sanction them” (11).

Rosenzweig’s turn to religion and away from a promising career in philosophy in the footsteps of Hegel, has much to do with his notion of time. Mosès delicately reconstructs the origins of Rosenzweig’s ideas on temporality through his letters with Eugen Rosenstock, an exchange that Mosès justifiably terms one of the most moving dialogues in Jewish-Christian relations. The debate concerning “chosenness” and “stubbornness”, two ideas integral to the notion of election, is perhaps the most revealing. Stubbornness, meaning the refusal to accept the belief in Jesus as Messiah, is entirely a category of Christian theology, says Rosenzweig, not an attribute of Jewishness. While Rosenstock sees election in Judaism as a relic of antiquity and as evidence of Judaism’s refusal to accept history and progress, Rosenzweig interprets “stubbornness” as part of the doctrine of election transferred to the Messiah that is an essential component of the concept of redemption in Christianity. The belief in a new election imbues Christianity with a fallacious sense of supremacy, he claims, and is also the source of a difficult asymmetry, for the role that Judaism plays in the doctrine of salvation and mission in Christianity has no counterpart in Judaism. Moreover, it is arguable whether the concept of election is a central article of Jewish faith, says Rosenzweig. We may add that in the rabbinic literature, no uniform doctrine of election can be easily discerned. Rather than theological, election takes on political and historical significance in Rosenzweig’s view. Modern European nationalism has sought to extend the doctrine of mission to worldly affairs with the aims of establishing “the complete Christianizing of the concept of people” under the ruse of secularism (29). Modernity comes to represent “the total secularization of Christianity or the total Christianization of Western civilization” (33). Election has an equally universalizing and unifying purpose in Judaism but no corresponding mission in history, he claims. It bears meaning only outside of history. It is here that Rosenzweig begins to formulate a two-fold conception of Judaism between its worldly existence and its inner transcendence, which was later to reappear in a central position inThe Star of Redemption. Set outside the politics of temporality, Jewish history represents a counterpoint to the secularized Messianism of modern nationalism, a meta-history which relies on an alternate view of the unfolding of events in a parallel current of sacred time.

Mosès attributes Rosenzweig’s concept of secularization to his critique of Hegel. Yet he would appear at times closer to Hegel than Mosès suggests. On one hand, Rosenzweig thoroughly repudiates the notion of history as the unfolding of reason. On the other hand, he maintains that secularization, or the Christianization of modernity, lays the groundwork for the unfolding of redemption. In a departure with most Jewish theology, Rosenzweig introduces an intimate relationship between the two religions, in which the advent of Christianity becomes a theological necessity forTheStar of Redemption. In his view, Judaism renounces temporal history and substitutes a meta-history of sacred time in its place, but it is still part of a wider experience in the life and death of nations. Judaism cannot be entirely beyond history here, as its own redemption is tied to the unfolding of Christianity, secularism, and modernity. Even if the Jewish people do not serve universal history, they still remain within the framework of temporal history. Mosès argues that by naming Hegel’s universalism a recent avatar of Christian history, Rosenzweig subverts the teleology of the system and places the notion of election in the league of modern nationalisms. However, in Rosenzweig’s view of Jewish history as experienced through the ritual symbolism of the cyclical year, it is difficult not to see a commonality with Hegel in the space between the categories and lived experience.

Here one may lodge notable differences between Rosenzweig and Benjamin. Mosès rightly illustrates how both authors shared an imminent concept of redemption and the ways in which Benjamin’s Messianism, as with other subjects, remain consistent throughout his lifetime. Mosès speaks of a model in Benjamin’s thought which is anti-sequential, exemplified by the conclusions to theOrigins of GermanTragic Dramathat “a work of art can never be deduced from those that precede it”. There is no history that follows unwaveringly from one advancing moment to the next, and no experience that is reducible to mere sequence, generalization, even totalization. Rather than a progression, history lies below layers of stratification (85). Redemption at any moment meant for Benjamin the search for a historical site between the incessant return of the unremarkable and an infinitely new that anticipates a complete and final end. Redemption was on no absolute course, symbolized by the last line of hisOn the Concept of History, which understands the immediacy of redemption as the door through which the Messiah may enter at any time. In addition to his own conceptual continuity in which, as “nothing is ever abandoned, everything is preserved”, Benjamin was equally concerned with continuity and causality in history (101). In the early years, he was indeed attracted to the systematic nature ofTheStar of Redemption, yet he would ultimately follow a course that was intrinsically methodical. He sought to avoid any theurgical impulse, favoring notions such as the “unintentional” of human acts which advances redemption without active causation. The delicate tightrope act that he performed between the free will of the individual and the necessity of redemption is characteristic of his approach. In the early years, this was presented in the guise of the Greek tragic hero who reverses his own fate by choosing the time and place of his own decline. In the later years, the tightrope is spanned across the interpretation of history, where each generation participates in a “weak messianic force” through the act of interpretation. It is not a Marxist end of history, based on qualitative and cumulative factors, and not a system in the sense ofThe Star of Redemption, but it remained a consistent focus of Benjamin’s political theology and vision of redemption.

Of the three figures, Scholem lived the longest, which would presumably offer greater opportunity to understand him. But I suspect Mosès’s general proximity to Scholem left less room for reflection. In the third section ofThe Angel of History, Scholem appears surely as one of the lesser angels in the shadow of the other two. He is not the remarkable figure that we have come to know through his journals and letters, which were published afterThe Angel of History, nor the quintessential scholar of Jewish thought who single-handedly transformed the study of Jews and Judaism today. HereThe Angel of Historyhas aged less well. Scholem’s inspiration is undeniable and the ideas that he developed together with Benjamin beginning in 1915 had a lasting impact on his work. The partnership reached its height in 1923 with Scholem’s departure for Jerusalem; Rosenzweig wasalready stricken with illness by then. Scholem’s political, theological and historical ideas continued to develop for fifty years. Mosès presents these years under a cloud of disappointments, extending Scholem’s discontent even to his scholarship of Jewish history. However, the more we understand of Scholem, the less we are inclined to see him as a nihilist who has retreated to a world of symbolic forms. Mosès claims that the young Scholem was initially drawn to Marxism, which is somewhat ironic since Scholem himself professed the same of Hannah Arendt, equally spuriously. In Scholem’s case, his journals and letters point unambiguously to a form of religious anarchism that was not too dissimilar from the German-Jewish circle that emigrated to Palestine in the early 1920s. At times Mosès seems to conflate Scholem’s scholarship with his religious anarchism. An example is his discussion of Messianism. Scholem’s Messianic idea is “always born of a historical frustration, it appears, in the collective consciousness, as compensation for a loss, as a utopian promise designed to make up for current misfortune” (131). It is a paradox, says Mosès, that the very existence of Messianism is predicated on its realization and yet its realization is its own abrogation. This phenomenon is then relayed to Zionism, since Zionism, he claims, “is the heir of Jewish Messianism” in Scholem’s view (133). He continues:

living in Jerusalem for more than ten years, he soon saw his ideal of a ‘spiritual’ Zionism devoted to the internal regeneration of the Jewish people collapse in the contact with the political reality of the country. In Scholem, this disillusion assumes the proportions of an authentic metaphysical crisis. His increasingly pronounced interest in heretical mystic doctrines combines with his political disenchantment to lead him close to a radical nihilism. (154)},
}

@article{tiedemann1983historical,
  author = {Tiedemann, Rolf},
  title = {Historical Materialism or Political Messianism? An Interpretation of the Theses ``On the Concept of History''},
  journal = {The Philosophical Forum},
  year = {1983},
  volume = {15},
  pages = {71--104},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Tiedemann, Benjamin's editor and close philological reader, argues that the Theses are irreducibly torn between historical materialism and political messianism—that the text does not resolve but performs this tension. He resists both purely materialist and purely theological readings and insists on the philological specificity of each thesis, tracing the sources (Marx, Blanqui, Brecht, Kraus, Hegel) that Benjamin cites within each.

  RELEVANCE: As the most rigorous philological reading of the Theses by Benjamin's own editor, this essay provides the benchmark for textual interpretation. It directly addresses the theological-materialist tension that structures Jetztzeit and the weak messianic power, and has defined the terms of the debate for subsequent scholarship.

  POSITION: Philological-materialist reading that insists on the unresolved tension between theology and Marxism.
  },
  keywords = {Benjamin, Theses-on-History, historical-materialism, messianism, philology, theology-materialism, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{torbidoni2021blasting,
  author = {Torbidoni, Juan},
  title = {Blasting the Continuum of History: Walter Benjamin's ``Now-Time'' and the Recovery of Experience},
  journal = {Thémata Revista de Filosofía},
  year = {2021},
  pages = {154--173},
  doi = {10.12795/THEMATA.2021.I63.09},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Torbidoni argues that Jetztzeit (now-time) should be understood not primarily as a political or theological category but as Benjamin's response to the Kantian devaluation of experience (Erfahrung): the concept of now-time ruptures homogeneous empty time in order to rescue the ephemeral moment as a metaphysically decisive instance of redemption. The opposition to ``homogeneous empty time'' is traced back to Benjamin's early concern with restoring the full weight of experience against Kantian formalism.

  RELEVANCE: This article connects the central temporal concepts of the Theses to Benjamin's early epistemological project, providing a philosophical genealogy for Jetztzeit that supplements both the theological and political genealogies. It directly addresses how now-time functions as a category of redemption and how it differs from mere chronological punctuality.

  POSITION: Philosophical reading connecting Jetztzeit to the early theory of experience and Kant critique.
  },
  keywords = {Benjamin, Jetztzeit, now-time, experience, Erfahrung, homogeneous-time, Medium},
  abstract = {This paper analyses the concept of “nowtime” (Jetztzeit) in Walter Benjamin’s “On the concept of history.” It shows its centrality in Benjamin’s philosophy of history, by defining it in opposition to two elements of Kantian philosophy: on the one hand, “empty, homogeneous time,” on the other, the faith in the infinite, inevitable progress of a generic “mankind.” It argues that the notions of now-time and truth as flitting hark back to Benjamin’s early concern about the devaluation of experience in modern philosophy and the need to rescue the ephemeral as a decisive element in metaphysics. Rather than a historical category, now-time denotes an instance of redemption},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{denysenko2021weak,
  author = {Denysenko, Anatoliy},
  title = {Walter Benjamin and the Weak Messianic Power},
  journal = {Theological Reflections: Eastern European Journal of Theology},
  year = {2021},
  volume = {19},
  pages = {70--88},
  doi = {10.29357/2789-1577.2021.19.2.70-88},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Denysenko surveys and synthesizes the main interpretive traditions surrounding Benjamin's concept of the weak messianic power, distinguishing its function from theological messianism and utopianism. He argues that the concept operates at the intersection of the tradition of the oppressed, the urgency of the ``now,'' and the model of collective memory (Eingedenken), and contrasts it with linear progressive conceptions of time.

  RELEVANCE: This article provides a useful synthetic overview of the weak messianic power concept, its relationship to Jetztzeit, and the range of scholarly interpretations, making it valuable as a secondary synthesis text that maps the debate around the core concepts of this domain.

  POSITION: Overview and synthesis of the interpretive debate around the weak messianic power.
  },
  keywords = {Benjamin, weak-messianic-power, Jetztzeit, Eingedenken, tradition-of-oppressed, messianic-time, Medium},
  abstract = {Walter Benjamin (1892-1940) was a German intellectual of Jewish descent, a well-known literary critic, philosopher, sociologist, translator and essayist, and a key figure in continental philosophy. His works on topics such as historical materialism, German idealism, and Jewish mysticism have had a marked influence on contemporary aesthetic theories and the development of Western Marxism, including the Frankfurt School of Critical Theory. These articles will focus on the analysis of the concept of messianism, which Benjamin develops in his work “On the concept of history” or “Theses on the philosophy of history” (1940). Messianism here is neither a theological dogma nor a modern figure of the utopian. Benjamin’s messianic time does not refer to the future, but to the urgency of the “now.” The author contrasts the “weak messianic force” of the tradition of the oppressed, which demands the past with the realization of happiness and liberation in the present, and Jetztzeit – a model of messianic time, open and nonlinear time of rupture, based on modern (contemporary) forms of collective experience past and liberation memory (Eingedenken).},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{inshakov2023concept,
  author = {Inshakov, I.},
  title = {The Concept of Messianic Time by Walter Benjamin and its Political Implications},
  journal = {Russian Sociological Review},
  year = {2023},
  doi = {10.17323/1728-192x-2023-1-29-47},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Inshakov applies an analytical approach to the concept of Jetztzeit, analyzing it through the related concepts of ``messianic time'' and ``kairos time,'' and traces the political implications of different interpretations: depending on how Jetztzeit is read, Benjamin appears either as a theorist of pure critique or as an advocate of direct revolutionary action. The article connects Jetztzeit to Benjamin's concept of divine violence and situates him against the ``mainstream'' Frankfurt School.

  RELEVANCE: This recent article directly examines the political stakes of interpreting Jetztzeit in different ways, which is a central issue for any synthesis of Benjamin's philosophy of history. Its analytical approach clarifies conceptual relations that are often left obscure in more literary readings.

  POSITION: Analytical-political reading that emphasizes the revolutionary implications of Jetztzeit.
  },
  keywords = {Benjamin, Jetztzeit, messianic-time, kairos, divine-violence, Frankfurt-School, Medium},
  abstract = {Despite Benjamin’s “renaissance” in contemporary research literature, the meaning of the concept of Jetztzeit(“Now-time”), one of his key political and philosophical concepts, remains vague, largely due to the tendency of researchers to follow Benjamin‘s narrative and metaphorical style. The author of this article purposely uses a strict analytical approach to capture the key characteristics of the concept and their relationship to each other. To do this, the concept is analyzed in the first part of the article through the prism of the related concepts of “messianic time” and “kairos time”. In the second part, the author traces the possible political implications of different interpretations of the concept itself and its connection with Benjamin’s concept of “divine violence”, showing that different interpretations lead to diametrically-opposite perceptions of Benjamin’s position, from a supporter of “pure criticism” to an activist of “direct actions”. In conclusion, the significant role of the Benjamin project as an alternative to the “mainstream” of the Frankfurt School (Theodor Adorno and Max Horkheimer) is pointed out in the context of today’s prospects for the revival of the Marxist political utopia.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@book{osborne1995politics,
  author = {Osborne, Peter},
  title = {The Politics of Time: Modernity and the Avant-Garde},
  year = {1995},
  publisher = {Verso},
  address = {London and New York},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Osborne situates Benjamin's theory of historical time within a broader philosophical account of modern temporality, arguing that Benjamin's concepts of now-time and the dialectical image constitute a distinctive ``politics of time'' that refuses both the teleological progressivism of Enlightenment historicism and the mere contemporaneity of postmodernism. Benjamin's temporal theory is read as part of the avant-garde response to the aporia of modernity's relationship to its own past.

  RELEVANCE: This book provides the most philosophically developed account of how Benjamin's Jetztzeit functions as a critical political concept within the modern philosophical tradition, situating it alongside Heidegger, Lukacs, and Adorno. It is essential background for understanding how Benjamin's rejection of homogeneous time connects to the broader crisis of modernity.

  POSITION: Critical-theoretical reading that situates Benjamin's temporal theory within the politics of modernity.
  },
  keywords = {Benjamin, modernity, avant-garde, temporal-politics, historicism, now-time, High},
  abstract = {In this ambitious book, Fiocco defends two remarkable theses. The first is the claim that the world is ontologically flat, “that each thing is fundamental; that reality has no ontological levels and […] that no thing is grounded in or made to be by another” (xv). The second is a version of the presentist thesis that nothing exists that is not present. While promoting presentism itself is not all that remarkable, Fiocco tries to make an unusually strong case. Rather than appeal to common sense or philosophical intuition, he claims to provide irrefutable, knock-down arguments for both of his theses.
  abstract_source = {ndpr},

These knock-down arguments are said to emerge from Fiocco’s fundamentalist approach to metaphysics, which he calls “original inquiry”. This inquiry is occasioned by confronting the world (“all this”), and it aims to answer the question “What is all this?” without taking anything for granted. Such a presuppositionless inquiry is said to permit us to “move past stalemate in metaphysical discussions” (22) and to lead us to the undeniable truth about time and the world, which includes Fiocco’s two theses.

To make progress with original inquiry, Fiocco thinks that we first need to address the fundamental ontological question “What is a thing?” (11). As he clarifies, “a thing is just an entity, an existent, a being” (60). Many philosophers will doubt that anything interesting can be said about things, in this general sense, that goes beyond the trivial observation that things are whatever quantifiers range over and singular terms denote. Fiocco disagrees. For him, elucidating the notion of thinghood is the most important question in metaphysics, and what ultimately leads to his two main theses.

Original inquiry provides Fiocco with the theoretical framework for a rich and wide-ranging discussion that spans almost the entire breadth of contemporary metaphysics. There is far more material in this book than one can hope to cover in a short review. Instead of saying a little bit about many different topics, I think it might be more helpful to focus on Fiocco’s main arguments for his two theses, which are contained in two very short passages on p. 77 and p. 179, respectively. The nothing-explains-anything argument on p. 77 promotes a radical restriction on the possibility of explanation; the farrago argument on p. 179 advocates a novel objection to eternalist thesis that past, present, and future are equally real.

My plan for this review is to give a detailed exposition of these two pivotal arguments, plus a quick survey of the rest of the book.

After spending the first seventy pages on a leisurely (and somewhat repetitive) exposition of original inquiry, Fiocco’s answer to the fundamental ontological question arises quite suddenly. On p. 73, he tells us that things are the ontological bases of explanations for the diversity in the world (“all this”). As it is given to us, the world is not uniform. The function of things is to explain this diversity. This might sound quite innocent, but the subsequent discussion reveals that Fiocco’s view is not that things can explain but that they must explain for them to count as things at all. It is not obvious how an original inquirer, who is not supposed to assume anything, arrives at this view. Why can’t there be terminal explananda that are explained by other things but do not explain anything themselves?

A few pages later, we discover that Fiocco holds a much more radical view. He does not merely deny the existence of terminal explananda, which would be the end points of chains of explanations, he claims that there cannot be any chains of explanation at all because no thing can be explained by any other thing:

If how an entity,e, [is] were explicable (in its entirety) in terms of some other thing,eitself would be ontologically idle, making no contribution per se to how the world is; such an “entity” would, if anything, merely be a manifestation of the latter, that genuine existent. Hence, if there were something that madeehow “it” is,e’s contribution to how the world is would be made by that thing that wholly determines or makes e howeis. Yet ife“itself” were not capable of contributing to a partial explanation for how all this is as it is, if “it” per se were insufficient to do at least this,ewould be no thing at all. “It” could in principle make no contribution to the world and, therefore, is literally, nothing. (77)},
}

@incollection{caygill2004cultural,
  author = {Caygill, Howard},
  title = {Walter Benjamin's Concept of Cultural History},
  booktitle = {The Cambridge Companion to Walter Benjamin},
  editor = {Ferris, David S.},
  year = {2004},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  address = {Cambridge},
  doi = {10.1017/ccol0521793297.005},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Caygill analyzes Benjamin's concept of cultural history as constituted by the interplay of the dialectical image and the concept of now-time, arguing that Benjamin transforms the Rankean model of historicism into a critical practice of ``reading'' historical constellations. The key claim is that cultural history for Benjamin is not the accumulation of facts but the construction of dialectical images that make the past politically legible in the present.

  RELEVANCE: This chapter provides a precise account of how the Theses connect to the Arcades Project and the broader concept of dialectical cultural history, and is particularly useful for understanding the concept of the constellation as the spatial form of Jetztzeit. It contextualizes the Theses within Benjamin's larger project.

  POSITION: Critical-materialist reading connecting the Theses to the Arcades Project methodology.
  },
  keywords = {Benjamin, cultural-history, constellation, dialectical-image, Jetztzeit, historicism, Medium},
  abstract = {There is no document of culture that is not at the same time a document of barbarism.Walter Benjamin, "Theses on the Philosophy of History"},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{andrew-benjamin2003roman,
  author = {Benjamin, Andrew},
  title = {Being Roman Now: The Time of Fashion. A Commentary on Walter Benjamin's `Theses on the Philosophy of History' XIV},
  journal = {Thesis Eleven},
  year = {2003},
  volume = {75},
  number = {1},
  pages = {39--53},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Andrew Benjamin offers a close reading of Thesis XIV—the tiger's leap into the past—focusing on the figure of fashion (Mode) as a mode of temporal citation. He argues that fashion models a form of repetition that is neither mere imitation nor dialectical progress but a citing of the past that is inherently political in its temporality. The Romans of the French Revolution did not merely imitate antiquity but performed a now-time citation that made the past politically available.

  RELEVANCE: This article provides one of the most detailed analyses of the Tigersprung figure and the concept of temporal citation that underlies Benjamin's model of revolutionary action. It is essential for understanding how the past can be made available to the present without being reduced to a model to imitate.

  POSITION: Philosophical reading that foregrounds the logic of citation and repetition in Benjamin's revolutionary temporality.
  },
  keywords = {Benjamin, tiger's-leap, fashion, Tigersprung, Thesis-XIV, temporal-citation, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{wohlfarth1978messianic,
  author = {Wohlfarth, Irving},
  title = {On the Messianic Structure of Walter Benjamin's Last Reflections},
  journal = {Glyph},
  year = {1978},
  volume = {3},
  pages = {148--212},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Wohlfarth's landmark essay argues that the Theses are structured by a constitutive and irresolvable tension between historical materialism and messianism—what he calls the ``messianic structure'' of Benjamin's last reflections. He reads this tension not as a contradiction to be resolved but as the productive paradox of a thought that refuses both triumphalist redemption and mechanical materialism, positing a ``standstill'' (Stillstand) as the moment of historical recognition.

  RELEVANCE: Wohlfarth's essay is the foundational text for understanding the messianic structure of the Theses, and his analysis of the Stillstand (dialectical standstill) as the structural form of Jetztzeit remains the reference point for subsequent scholarship. It is indispensable for any treatment of the now-time concept.

  POSITION: Messianic-dialectical reading that insists on the productive irresolvability of the theology-materialism tension.
  },
  keywords = {Benjamin, messianic-structure, Stillstand, Theses-on-History, historical-materialism, dialectics, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{ostovich1994messianic,
  author = {Ostovich, Steven T.},
  title = {Messianic History in Benjamin and Metz},
  journal = {Philosophy and Theology},
  year = {1994},
  volume = {8},
  number = {4},
  pages = {271--289},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Ostovich compares Benjamin's messianic conception of history to Johann Baptist Metz's political theology, arguing that both insist on the interruptive capacity of dangerous memories of suffering against the narrative of linear progress. For both thinkers, history is not the record of progress through empty time but a site of eschatological promise and catastrophe. Ostovich also notes where Metz's theological categories offer resources that avoid the dualism in Benjamin's dual (messianic and materialist) account of history.

  RELEVANCE: This comparative article illuminates the theological dimension of Benjamin's messianism by placing it within the tradition of political theology, and helps clarify what is distinctively philosophical (rather than confessional) about Benjamin's claim that history must be conceived messianically. The contrast with Metz is useful for understanding the limits and commitments of Benjamin's concept.

  POSITION: Theological-comparative reading placing Benjamin within the tradition of political theology.
  },
  keywords = {Benjamin, Metz, messianic-history, political-theology, suffering, interruption, Low, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{thate2016messianic,
  author = {Thate, Michael J.},
  title = {Messianic Time and Monetary Value},
  journal = {Religions},
  year = {2016},
  volume = {7},
  number = {9},
  pages = {112},
  doi = {10.3390/REL7090112},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Thate reads Benjamin's messianic time as a ``divestment'' from historical time's constitution of identities within late capitalism: messianism represents the possibility of bringing asymmetrical accumulation and the monetary value of market time to a standstill. The article pushes Benjamin's concept of now-time toward a critique of financialization and the temporal structures of global capital.

  RELEVANCE: This article provides a contemporary application of Jetztzeit to political economy that illuminates the anti-capitalist dimension of Benjamin's temporal theory, connecting the critique of homogeneous empty time to the critique of capitalist time as the time of exchange value. Useful for understanding the intersection of messianic and materialist registers in the Theses.

  POSITION: Political-economic reading that connects messianic time to the critique of capitalist temporality.
  },
  keywords = {Benjamin, messianic-time, capitalism, monetary-value, Jetztzeit, political-economy, Low},
  abstract = {In this essay we return to Walter Benjamin’s notion of messianic time as outlined in his Theses on the Philosophy of History. Messianic time is read with Benjamin’s Sonnette as a “divestment” from historical time. That is, messianic time is a relinquishing of historical time’s formation of identities within late capitalism. Messianism represents that opening which whispers the possibility of bringing asymmetrical accumulation and subjective formation to a standstill. The aim of the essay is thus to push a rereading of Benjamin’s notion of messianic time as subjective divestment from historical time which in turn breaks the uneven distribution of time, accumulation, and the monetary value of market time at work in our current world of global finance.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}
